
  Only 3–6 are the notifications system. 1 and 2 are the app it sits on. I'd rather build these as separate spec → plan
  → implementation cycles than pretend they're one feature, and the order matters: you can't design "changes to tasks
  you follow" before "task" and "you" exist.

  So the question that decides nearly everything downstream:

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether a second human ever sees your tasks. That determines if this needs a server and accounts, or can be
  a self-contained browser app.

  - [1mSingle-user, local[0m — tasks live in the browser (localStorage/IndexedDB). "Notifications" become due-date reminders
    and self-set alerts. No backend, no accounts, no sync. Cheap, fast, and genuinely useful — but it rules out anyone
    notifying [3myou[0m about anything, and retrofitting multi-user later means rewriting the storage layer.
  - [1mMulti-user, shared backend[0m — real accounts, a server, tasks assigned between people. "Notifications" become the real
    thing: someone reassigned your task, commented, changed the due date. This is where the feature is actually
    interesting, and it's roughly 4–5× the work: auth, database, API, plus the notification pipeline.
  - [1mMulti-user, fake auth[0m — full multi-user data model and notification semantics, but you pick a user from a dropdown
    instead of logging in. Gets the interesting design work without burning the project on auth plumbing. Auth slots in
    later without touching the notification design.

  [1mMy recommendation: multi-user with fake auth.[0m Your phrasing — "users," plural, "tasks they care about" — describes
  collaboration, and a single-user reminder system wouldn't need the word "notified" at all. But real auth is the least
  interesting and most replaceable part, so I'd stub it and spend the design effort on the event model.
